The family records the package that built it: the namespace of the
function that called \code{frmtmb_family()}, read with
\code{\link[=frm_family_package]{frm_family_package()}}, and \code{"frmtmb"} for a family built outside
any package. A saved fit keeps that record. When the fit is read
back in a session where that package is not installed, frmtmb stops
at the first method that needs the family's functions (\code{summary()},
\code{vcov()}, \code{predict()}, \code{fitted()}, \code{simulate()}, \code{update()},
\code{\link[=frm_curve]{frm_curve()}} and the rest) with a message that names the package,
rather than with the "could not find function" of one of its
internals. A \code{\link[=mixture]{mixture()}} records the packages of its components
the same way.

\code{$} does not partial-match on a family object. \code{fam$lpd} is an
error naming the field it would have matched, rather than
silently returning it. Use the full name.
}
\description{
Constructs a family object for use with \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}}. The log-density
function must be vectorized and AD-compatible: it is evaluated on RTMB
'advector' objects during taping, so it must use RTMB-overloaded
operations (RTMB and RTMBdist \verb{d*} functions, plain arithmetic) and must
not branch on parameter values.
}
\section{Structured simulators}{

Some families cannot draw a response one row at a time: a group-level
\code{\link[=mixture]{mixture()}} draws one class per group, a multivariate normal mixture
(frmtmb.latent's \code{mixture_mvn()}) needs the class covariances, which
are family-level extras rather than dpars, and a hidden Markov family
walks a chain per sequence. Those
families supply \code{sim_ctx(ctx)} instead of \code{sim(dpars, aterms, n)}.

\code{ctx} is a list with \code{fit} (any object carrying \code{spec}, \code{frame} and
\code{estimates} - a fitted model, one posterior draw, or the de novo
shim), \code{family}, \code{rspec}, \code{resp}, \code{dpars} (the evaluated numeric
distributional parameters), \code{aterms}, \code{n}, \code{extra} (the family-level
extra parameters) and the frame structures \code{autocor} and \code{block}
(the structured family's own data; see \code{\link[=frmtmb_structure]{frmtmb_structure()}}).
Read its fields with \verb{[[ ]]}.

The same \code{sim_ctx()} serves [simulate()], \code{posterior_predict()} and
[frm_simulate()]. Because a structured draw covers whole sequences or
groups, \code{trunc()} rejection and \code{newdata} cannot apply to it and are
refused.

[ ]: R:\%20
[simulate()]: R:simulate()
[frm_simulate()]: R:frm_simulate()
}

\section{Slot call order}{

The order the slots run in is part of the contract, because a family
that derives anything from the data depends on it. Measured on an
instrumented family, one fit of one response:
\enumerate{
\item \code{valid_y(y, aterms)}, once, at frame assembly, with the response
coerced and the addition terms evaluated.
\item \code{family_finalize(fam, y, aterms)}, once, immediately after, and
still before any link function is called.
\item \code{aterm_data()} and \code{extra_pars(y, aterms)}, once each, at
assembly.
\item \code{init_dpars[[dpar]](y, aterms)}, once per dpar, when the starting
values are built; each value goes straight through that dpar's
\code{linkfun}.
\item \code{linkinv} and \code{lpdf}, on the tape, from then on.
}

\code{post$mean_fn}, \code{post$var_fn}, \code{post$dev_fn} and \code{sim} are never
called by \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}}. They run only when a post-fit method asks for
them.

So a link may read anything \code{valid_y} or \code{family_finalize} computed,
and neither of those may read anything a link produced.
}

\section{Deriving a family from the data}{

Some families are not fully determined until the response is in hand.
A shifted family whose density is zero below a non-decision time
wants a link bounded above by \code{min(y)}, so that the constraint is
structural rather than left to the optimizer. The bound is a property
of the data, and the family object is built before \code{\link[=frm]{frm()}} sees any.

\code{family_finalize} closes that gap. It receives the family, the
coerced response and the evaluated addition terms, and returns the
family the fit will use:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{shifted <- frmtmb_family(
  "shifted", dpars = c("mu", "ndt"),
  links = list(mu = "log", ndt = "log"),
  lpdf = function(y, dpars, aterms) \{ ... \},
  family_finalize = function(fam, y, aterms) \{
    # a logit onto (0, min(y)): ndt cannot reach the smallest
    # observation, whatever the optimizer tries
    ub <- min(y)
    fam$links$ndt <- list(
      name    = paste0("scaled_logit(0, ", signif(ub, 4), ")"),
      linkfun = function(mu) log(mu / (ub - mu)),
      linkinv = function(eta) ub / (1 + exp(-eta)),
      mu_eta  = function(eta) \{
        p <- 1 / (1 + exp(-eta))
        ub * p * (1 - p)
      \}
    )
    fam
  \}
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Replacing a link this way replaces it everywhere: the starting
values, the tape, and every post-fit method read the finalized
family. The alternative an extension reached for before this slot
existed was to have \code{valid_y} write the bound into an environment the
link closures read at call time, which works only for as long as the
call order happens to hold and leaves the family object lying about
what it is.
}

\section{Censoring and the representable tail}{

For a family that declares \code{cdf_log}, every censored row and every
truncation window is the mass of a window \verb{(lo, hi]} (\code{row_lpdf()}),
and core forms it on the log scale from both tails of the family's
CDF: from \verb{lS = log S} where \code{S(lo) >= 1/2}, as
\code{lS(lo) + log(1 - exp(lS(hi) - lS(lo)))}, and from \verb{lF = log F} below
that. A log near zero keeps every digit of its
complement, so each form keeps the digits the other loses. A window
open above is \code{lS(lo)} and one open below is \code{lF(hi)}, so a
right-censored row and \code{trunc(lb = )} read the log survivor itself.

The probability scale cannot do this. A double cannot represent the
complement of a probability that has rounded to one, so \code{log(1 - F)}
past that point is not merely inaccurate: it is CONSTANT, and its
gradient is exactly zero. An optimizer then prices such a row the
same however far it moves. That failure is silent: the fit converges,
and \code{logLik()} and \code{AIC()} report the floored number. Measured on a
standard normal tail, one process:

\tabular{lrr}{
\strong{z} \tab \strong{log(1 - pnorm(z))} \tab
\strong{pnorm(z, lower.tail = FALSE, log.p = TRUE)} \cr
8.0 \tab -35.013 \tab -35.013 \cr
8.3 \tab -Inf \tab -37.494 \cr
37 \tab -Inf \tab -689.031 \cr
500 \tab -Inf \tab -125007.13
}

Every built-in family with a CDF declares \code{cdf_log} except
\code{\link[=exponential]{exponential()}}, \code{\link[=weibull]{weibull()}}, \code{\link[=frechet]{frechet()}},
\code{\link[=gen_extreme_value]{gen_extreme_value()}} and \code{\link[=discrete_weibull]{discrete_weibull()}}. A family without it
is read through \code{lcdf} and \code{lccdf} as before: a right-censored row is
\code{lccdf} (or \code{log(1 - lcdf)} without it), a left-censored row
\code{log(F(y) - F(lb))}, an interval \code{log(F(y2) - F(y))}, the window
\code{log(F(ub) - F(lb))}, and \code{trunc(lb = )} with no upper bound
\code{lccdf(lb)}. Each slot is called where it always was, because a
family may read the censoring codes to know which rows a call is
for, as the frmtmb.eam Wiener families do, and may supply an \code{lcdf}
that is not the complement of its \code{lccdf}. The probability scale
ends at \code{1e-308}, below which a left-censored row is \code{log(0)}, and an
interval in an upper tail loses its digits; a family that declares
\code{cdf_log} has neither limit.

The built-in \code{cdf_log} pairs compute the tail on the far side of \code{q}
directly, as an integral or a series whose terms are all positive:
the incomplete beta for \code{\link[=student]{student()}}, \code{\link[=Beta]{Beta()}}, \code{\link[=binomial]{binomial()}} and
\code{\link[=negbinomial]{negbinomial()}}, the incomplete gamma for \code{\link[=Gamma]{Gamma()}} and for \code{\link[=poisson]{poisson()}}
past a count of 30 (a smaller count is a finite sum of its masses),
Owen's \code{T} as an angle integral for \code{\link[=skew_normal]{skew_normal()}}, the density's
own angle integral for \code{\link[=von_mises]{von_mises()}}, and \code{pnorm()}'s two log tails
for \code{\link[=gaussian]{gaussian()}} and \code{\link[=lognormal]{lognormal()}}. \code{\link[=inverse.gaussian]{inverse.gaussian()}} and
\code{\link[=exgaussian]{exgaussian()}} are log-scale differences. A zero-inflated or hurdle
family is built from its base family's pair. Two are complements in
part of the range: \link[=brmsfamily]{brmsfamily("com_poisson")}, whose
support has no top, takes its survivor as the complement of the
summed CDF until that is below about \code{1e-3} and as an upper sum
beyond; and a hurdle family takes \code{F} as the complement of \code{S} where
\code{S < 1/2}.

\code{RTMB::ppois(lower.tail = FALSE, log.p = TRUE)} and
\code{RTMB::pgamma()}'s upper tail do not tape (inside \code{MakeADFun} they
reach \code{stats::ppois()} and \code{stats::pgamma()} and stop at
"Non-numeric argument to mathematical function"), which is why
poisson had no \code{lccdf} through frmtmb 0.72.0; its survivor is exact
now (\code{-2773.28} at \code{q = 700}, \code{lambda = 5}, where \code{log(1 - F)} is
\code{-Inf}).
}

\section{Censoring a discrete response}{

A discrete response's bounds are read as brms 2.23.0 reads them:

\tabular{lll}{
\strong{term} \tab \strong{means} \tab \strong{scored as} \cr
\code{cens()} \code{0} "none" \tab \code{Y == y} \tab \code{f(y)} \cr
\code{cens()} \code{-1} "left" \tab \code{Y <= y} \tab \code{F(y)} \cr
\code{cens()} \code{1} "right" \tab \code{Y > y} \tab \code{S(y)} \cr
\code{cens()} \code{2} "interval" \tab \verb{y < Y <= y2} \tab \code{F(y2) - F(y)} \cr
\code{trunc(lb, ub)} \tab \verb{lb <= Y <= ub} \tab \code{F(ub) - F(lb - 1)} \cr
\code{trunc(lb)} \tab \code{Y >= lb} \tab \code{S(lb - 1)}
}

A censoring bound is the edge of a continuous-style window \verb{(lo, hi]},
and only the truncation lower bound is inclusive. \code{trunc(lb = 0)}, or
any \code{lb <= 0} on a count, is no truncation and is accepted, as it is
in brms. Every bound must be a whole number: a fractional one would be
read as its floor. An interval whose ends agree (\code{y2 == y}) is an
event of probability zero and is refused, as on a continuous
response; an exact observation is code 0. To score "6 or more",
right censor at 5.

Under \code{trunc()} a censored row is the event inside the window, on any
response: right censoring at \code{y} is \verb{P(y < Y <= ub)} and left
censoring \verb{P(lb <= Y <= y)}, each over the window's mass. brms 2.23.0
divides \code{P(Y > y)} or \code{P(Y <= y)}, the event over the whole line, by
the window's mass, which can exceed one, so the two differ where a
censored event reaches past a bound: right censoring with an upper
bound, left censoring with a lower one. With no bound on that side,
and for every interval, they agree.

\code{simulate(censored = TRUE)} records a draw above a right-censoring
point \code{k} as \code{k}, and a draw at \code{k} is the uncensored value \code{k}, so
the simulated values are the same under either reading of a bound.

\code{residuals(type = "osa")} is refused on a censored discrete fit: an
uncensored row's support is \verb{[lo + 1, hi]} rather than the \verb{[lo, hi]}
the one-step window is built on.
}

\section{Tape-safe scope}{

\code{lpdf} and \code{lcdf} run with RTMB's tape-safe \code{c()}, \verb{[<-} and
\verb{diag<-} in scope automatically (the
\code{"c" <- RTMB::ADoverload("c")} boilerplate is spliced in unless the
function already binds it), so base spellings keep the
automatic-differentiation class. A helper the density CALLS still
needs its own bindings: lexical scope does not travel into other
functions.
}

\examples{
# a custom family is a plain R log-density over taped parameters
dd <- data.frame(y = rbinom(100, 5, 0.4),
                 size = 5, x = rnorm(100))
fam <- custom_family(
  "vbinom", dpars = "mu", links = list(mu = "logit"),
  lpdf = function(y, dpars, aterms) {
    RTMB::dbinom(y, aterms$vint1, dpars$mu, log = TRUE)
  },
  # the density indexes vint1, so a model without it is refused
  # rather than fitted against a zero-length log-likelihood
  required_aterms = "vint1",
  type = "discrete"
)
fit <- frm(bf(y | vint(size) ~ x) + fam, data = dd)
fixef(fit)
}
\seealso{
\link{frmtmb-robust-dpars} for the accessors a density uses to
stay exact where an inverse link saturates,
\code{\link[=frmtmb_structure]{frmtmb_structure()}} for a likelihood that does not
factorize over rows, \code{\link[=frmtmb_register_aterm]{frmtmb_register_aterm()}} for giving the
family's per-row data a name of its own instead of \code{vint()},
\code{\link[=frmtmb_register_compat]{frmtmb_register_compat()}} for telling \code{\link[=frm_compat]{frm_compat()}} what the
family does and does not combine with, and
\link{frmtmb-extension-api} for the accessors a family outside frmtmb
may use after a fit
}
